  Ukrainian capital of Kharkov but those  in the countryside ie those who grew the  food were left with little interestingly  those who had previously been deported  to forced labor camps were often given  larger daily rations than those who  worked on the farms or lived in the  cities Lazar Kaganovich Stalin's second  in command ordered stanislav castillo  the leader of the ukrainian communist  party to crack down on the peasantry the  government was concerned that starving  Ukrainians fleeing Ukraine with  demoralizing the rest of the USSR and  would make collectivization and the  Stalin look bad as such the government  chose to pull back on collectivization  in ukrainian just kidding they bound  people from moving throughout the USSR  and denied that any famine was happening  furthermore any discussion surrounding  starvation was seen through the lens of  being a hunger strike orchestrated by  Polish spies and Ukrainian nationalists  also during the famine the Soviet Union  was still exporting grain in simply  halting this or opening up the nation's  extensive grain reserves would have  alleviated a great deal of the suffering  Stalin didn't want to though since the  money raised from selling grain was used  to build heavy industry across the  nation this period known as the  Holodomor  or the terra famine continued until 1933  when crop yields began to increase and  were able to feed the now much lower  population estimates vary with the death  toll of the famine in Ukraine is  considered by historians to be at about  roughly 3 million in fact when the USSR  conducted a census in 1937 the figures  for the population were lower than  anticipated and so Stalin had those who  compiled it shocked the big debate  surrounding this event is whether or not  it should be labeled as the genocide and  to what extent who was responsible not  however is a debate for another video I  hope you enjoyed this episode and thank  you for watching and a special thanks to  all of the